    description: "Found while investigating why `output-capture-long-running-playbook` kept getting re-injected despite a 100K-token dedup window. Root cause applies to ALL playbooks, not just output-capture.\n\n## Symptom\n\nPlaybooks get repeatedly injected into the agent's context within the dedup window that `on_transcript_change.py` claims to honor. Measured this session (session `2ffda1ad-bf65-4f5c-ade6-e3bcf8610db6`, Claude Opus, ~2.5h):\n\n| Playbook | Total injections | Violations (within dedup window) | Smallest inter-injection gap |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| output-capture-long-running-playbook | 9 | 5 | 0.4s |\n| pre-work-playbook | 6 | 3 | 2.3s |\n| smart-test-playbook | 6 | 4 | 0.5s |\n| optional-dependency-testing-playbook | 3 | 2 | 1.2s |\n| recover-state-playbook | 3 | 1 | 1193.6s |\n| vpr-usage | 2 | 1 | 22.7s |\n| **Session-wide total** | **49** | **16** | — |\n\n**Session-wide violation rate: 16/49 = 33%.** Every playbook with a sub-3-second inter-injection gap had violations. Playbooks that only saw one injection or had time gaps in the hundreds/thousands of seconds had none. The correlation between \"sub-3-second gap\" and \"violation\" is 1:1 — a textbook concurrent-invocation signature.\n\nDedup window is `DEDUP_TOKENS = 100,000` tokens × `CHARS_PER_TOKEN = 4.4` = **440,000 characters**. All observed violations had deltas under 36,000 characters from the nearest prior injection of the same playbook — deep inside the window.\n\n## Root cause\n\n`.agent/hooks/_shared/on_transcript_change.py:637-673` — no locking around the load → decide → save sequence on the per-session injection-state file:\n\n```python\ndef load_injection_state(repo_root: str, session_id: str) -> dict:\n    # reads .agent/.transcript-injection-state.<sid>.json, returns dict\n    ...\n\ndef save_injection_state(repo_root: str, session_id: str, state: dict) -> None:\n    # writes tmp file then os.replace(tmp, path)\n    ...\n```\n\n`os.replace` gives atomic **rename** (final writer wins the file), but there's no `flock` across the check-then-act window. When the agent makes parallel Bash tool calls (or any other parallel tool use), each tool completion fires its own `PostToolUse` hook, the hooks invoke `on_transcript_change.py` concurrently, they all `load_injection_state()` before any has written, they all decide \"OC not recently injected, select it\", each emits the playbook to its own hook-output stream — and both injections land in the agent's context.\n\nRace window:\n\n```\nHook A                          Hook B\n------                          ------\nload_injection_state()          load_injection_state()   ← both read state BEFORE any write\nselector picks OC               selector picks OC\nemit OC injection               emit OC injection         ← both injections land\nsave_injection_state(A)         save_injection_state(B)   ← lost-update on the state file\n```\n\nThe atomicity of `os.replace` prevents file corruption but doesn't prevent the duplicate injections — those already happened in the agent's context by the time either save runs.\n\n## Impact\n\nThree compounding effects:\n\n1. **Context pollution.** A 33% violation rate means roughly a third of all playbook injections this session were redundant. Each redundant injection burns ~3-5K chars of the agent's context window on content the selector LLM believed was already present.\n\n2. **Agent reasoning bias.** Repeated injections of the same failure-mode framings make those failure modes over-salient during diagnosis. Motivating incident (dogfood round 2026-04-24 same session): when tracker ops briefly disappeared, this agent attributed the loss to the WI-buhov / auto-pr stash-loss bug mentioned in `output-capture-long-running-playbook`'s \"Special Hazards\" section. That playbook had been injected 5 times in the prior 90 minutes (4 of which were dedup violations from this race). The agent asserted the WI-buhov diagnosis with more confidence than evidence supported; root cause was more likely this same race affecting tracker auto-sync's state updates, not WI-buhov. The user (human) had to challenge the reasoning explicitly for the bias to be acknowledged.\n\n3. **Summary-side priming.** The playbook summaries (lines 158-320 of on_transcript_change.py) include specific incident IDs like `WI-buhov` and `WI-kujis` in their summary text. When the selector LLM sees those IDs repeatedly in the PLAYBOOKS list and the matching playbook gets injected redundantly, the agent's retrieval gets disproportionately primed toward those specific handles. Separate (lower-priority) follow-up for that: neutralise the summary wording.\n\n## Fix shape\n\nPrecedent: `WI-sinap` (done P2) added `flock` to `scripts/smart-test` for exactly this reason — concurrent invocations corrupting shared state. And `INV-filaj` (satisfied P2) codifies the broader invariant that concurrent sessions must not corrupt each other's transcript/watcher state.\n\nApply the same `fcntl.flock` pattern at the `load_injection_state` → `save_injection_state` boundary in `on_transcript_change.py`:\n\n```python\nimport fcntl\n\ndef _lock_path(repo_root: str, session_id: str) -> str:\n    return _state_path(repo_root, session_id) + \".lock\"\n\ndef with_injection_state_lock(repo_root, session_id):\n    \"\"\"Context manager: exclusive flock around state read-modify-write.\"\"\"\n    lock_path = _lock_path(repo_root, session_id)\n    os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(lock_path), exist_ok=True)\n    lock_fd = open(lock_path, \"w\")\n    try:\n        fcntl.flock(lock_fd.fileno(), fcntl.LOCK_EX)\n        yield\n    finally:\n        fcntl.flock(lock_fd.fileno(), fcntl.LOCK_UN)\n        lock_fd.close()\n```\n\nThen in `main()`, wrap the existing flow:\n\n```python\nwith with_injection_state_lock(repo_root, session_id):\n    state = load_injection_state(...)\n    already, state = recently_injected(...)\n    if len(already) == len(PLAYBOOKS):\n        sys.exit(0)\n    # ... steps 1-3 ...\n    # After decision, update state.injections and save\n    save_injection_state(..., state)\n```\n\nThe lock is per-session, so it doesn't serialise across concurrent repos or users. It DOES serialise concurrent hook fires within one session, which is the intended behaviour — hook B waits for hook A's inject-or-skip decision to land before making its own.\n\nCost: hooks will wait on the flock when they race. Expected wait is milliseconds (the critical section is mostly file I/O and LLM API calls — but the LLM calls should be outside the lock). Design carefully: hold the lock long enough to read state and decide dedup, release it before the LLM calls, re-acquire to commit the injection record.\n\nAlternative: commit-and-check with a write-wins semantic that still loses injections but not history (record every injection attempt in a separate append-only ledger; dedup by querying the ledger). More complex; stick with `flock`.\n\n## Scope expansion\n\nPer the Structural Fix protocol:\n\n- **Other shared state files in `.agent/hooks/_shared/`?** Likely yes — any file read then written by the PostToolUse hook family has the same vulnerability. Audit candidates: `stop_hook_state.json`, `agent_notes.json` (though `agent_notes.json` is single-writer via the `scripts/agent-notes` tool per the AGENTS.md guidance, so may be safe if only that tool writes). Quick grep for `save_*` + `load_*` patterns in the hooks directory will enumerate.\n- **Tracker auto-sync's internal state?** The failure I chased earlier (WI-nunuj / WI-davan items disappearing after creation) may share this root cause — tracker auto-sync's backup-restore cycle could be the same TOCTOU on a different file. Worth verifying separately.\n- **Invariant candidate:** \"Any read-modify-write on shared state from a hook or tool that may run concurrently MUST use `fcntl.flock` (or an equivalent advisory lock) across the critical section.\" Tag WI-sinap and INV-filaj as precedent; this item and follow-ups as same-family.\n\n## Validation\n\nAfter fix:\n1. Reproduce the race locally by writing a test fixture that invokes `on_transcript_change.py` twice in parallel with `time.sleep`-gated read/write.\n2. Check injection-history logs of a subsequent agent session for violation-rate metric (should drop to ~0%).\n3. Observe whether the agent's tendency to over-reference specific playbook incident IDs (like WI-buhov in this session's diagnosis) diminishes — weaker empirical claim; tag `awaits_bakeoff_validation` at merge time if we want this tracked.\n\n## Related / precedent items\n\n- `WI-sinap` (done P2): smart-test flock guard — direct precedent for the fix pattern\n- `INV-filaj` (satisfied P2): concurrent sessions invariant — broader invariant this rolls up under\n- `WI-lufal` (done P1): tracker auto-sync failed-push should not discard local ops — adjacent concurrency concern, different mechanism\n- (new follow-up suggested) Neutralise summary wording in PLAYBOOKS to reduce priming bias — tag reduces from structural to cosmetic once the race is fixed but remains a latent issue"  # 9337
